  "contributors": "Pang supplied the PAL/RPL/BLA direction to preserve labels, compare costs on one board and attempt both orientations. Hearthline derived the Pascal allocation, matched share and inversion control. Lagarias and Mehta previously studied the Pascal product and its valuations; Du and Lagarias studied its partial factorizations.",
  "formal_scope": "Universal two-packet inequality, exact factorization at each prime above the square root, all-size storage for matched prime factors, coprime remainders and comparison-to-full-large-prime funding, exact 175-state residual at 16, and incompatible positive row readings at one sixth and one fifth.",
  "paper_scope": "Same-prime matches pay a limiting fraction 0.6164286796... of logarithmic input across large prime labels. For each fixed finite group of leading cofactor bands the Pascal factors of that same group eventually suffice. A positive mixture carrying each prime in its own radix must exceed the leading one-direction budget by at least one part in 5500. The remaining unrestricted coprime comparison at all late fourth powers is RH-equivalent.",
  "review": {
    "type": "author self-review",
    "fit": "A_n=n^n/n! has nondecreasing growth factors. Pairing opposite row sizes pays P_n squared without a hypothesis about primes. The implementation uses reversible mixed-radix rank.",
    "exact_carries": "The formula q*(p-1-r) holds when n<p^2. Each binomial coefficient is tested independently at the small heads.",
    "gross_vs_net": "Positive and negative cell areas both have the same leading total. Their leading equality does not compare the two remaining integers.",
    "dual": "Möbius inversion and an elementary recovered tail give the target weighted tent value -1/11. Its dual norm is below 1000. Uniform bounded variation on [e,1] transfers the lower bound to actual prime observations before e tends to zero.",
    "scope": "The obstruction is for independently charged, nonnegative Pascal subrows with p-wise funding, not arbitrary joint encodings."
  },
